

ADS‑ESS: A QCD‑Structured Dynamic Stability Engine for High‑Energy Simulation

Abstract

The Astromorphic Dynamic Stability Engine Simulation Software (ADS‑ESS) is a quantum‑chromodynamic (QCD)–aware computational framework designed to model dynamic stability, confinement behavior, and color‑field evolution within high‑energy systems. ADS‑ESS integrates a QCD topology kernel, a color‑field particle‑in‑cell (PIC) engine, confinement‑pressure stabilization, dynamic topological connectivity tracking, and suprakernel validation. This work formalizes the mathematical foundations, computational architecture, and stability mechanisms underlying ADS‑ESS, positioning it as a foundational substrate for proton‑structured propulsion systems and QCD‑driven simulation environments.

1. Introduction

High‑energy systems such as proton‑structured propulsion engines require simulation frameworks capable of modeling stability at the quantum chromodynamic level. Traditional electromagnetic PIC engines lack the ability to represent confinement dynamics, color‑field evolution, and topological stability. ADS‑ESS addresses this gap by embedding QCD structure directly into the simulation substrate.

The system models quark–gluon topology, confinement pressure, dynamic connectivity, and color‑field evolution. It further integrates a suprakernel validation layer to ensure physical consistency and computational integrity.

ADS‑ESS is designed as a scientific substrate rather than a conventional simulation tool, enabling proton‑scale stability modeling for advanced propulsion concepts and high‑energy physics research.

2. QCD Topology Kernel

